  "transcript": "# Biosecurity and animal welfare\n\n## Immediate impacts on farms and vets\n> Bluetongue caused serious animal illness and deaths, financial losses, and heavy emotional strain on farmers and vets.\n\nChair: We’ll start with the recent reports of Shamonda virus and how it relates to bluetongue—any immediate thoughts?\nDr Rees: Shamonda is worrying and new to the UK. It was expected eventually; its detection shows surveillance is working, but there’s a lot we don’t yet know.\nCat McLaughlin: Farmers are concerned. Many won’t know about Shamonda, there’s no easy control or treatment, and it’s another thing farmers must manage financially and emotionally.\nChair: Cat, broadly, what did bluetongue do to farms this year—financially, for welfare, and for the sector?\nCat McLaughlin: Losses included deaths, reproductive failure, milk and growth drops, extra vet bills and labour, plus delayed impacts on next year’s lambing and calving; emotional stress was severe.\nDr Rees: Clinically we saw much sicker animals and higher mortality, especially in southwest sheep. Vets could only offer supportive care—no direct treatments—and fertility impacts are a major worry.\nChair: Did changes in the veterinary profession affect response capability on the ground?\nDr Rees: Private vets worked extremely hard and weren’t absent, but the profession’s demographics mean fewer farm-experienced vets than in 2001, so broader preparedness needs work.\nCat McLaughlin: Farmers often have preventive relationships with private vets now, which helped, but in areas with fewer vets that can stretch capacity during big outbreaks.\n\n\n## Testing, reporting and communications\n> Delays and mid-outbreak policy changes slowed identification and undermined confidence; better, digitised reporting and clearer messaging are needed.\n\nSarah Bool: Despite #BattleBluetongue and warnings, many farmers didn’t vaccinate early—why didn’t the message stick?\nDr Rees: Communicating uncertain risk is hard. Vaccines cost money, margins are tight, and early on vets had limited efficacy data for the new vaccine—many balanced cost vs uncertain benefit.\nCat McLaughlin: Misinformation on social media made things harder. The industry-made bluetongue hub helped, but cost, supply problems and timing still stopped earlier uptake.\nHenry Tufnell: There were three-week test delays in August. What effect did that have?\nDr Rees: Maps of confirmed cases were two to three weeks out of date, so suspicion and real-time risk weren’t shown. Publishing suspected cases later fixed that and helped local conversations.\nCat McLaughlin: Some farmers waited much longer than three weeks for lab results in the southwest. That uncertainty increased stress and complicated decision-making on farms.\nHenry Tufnell: There was a shift to allow private vets to report clinically rather than waiting for tests. Was that handled well?\nDr Rees: The move made sense in context, but it was communicated with very short notice—about 36 hours—so vets felt unprepared and worried about missing something like foot and mouth.\nChair: Different devolved policies paid for testing differently. Does that cause problems?\nCat McLaughlin: Yes—exotic diseases don’t respect borders. England’s farmers paying for official tests while Wales and Scotland supported tests feels inequitable; better cross-border reasoning is needed.\n\n\n## Vaccines and supply chains\n> Vaccine stock followed demand; limited shelf life, pack sizes and forecasting gaps caused shortages—better forecasting and industry engagement are essential.\n\nCharlie Dewhirst: Europe used lots of vaccine early this year. How do we avoid a repeat in 2027?\nDr Rees: Manufacturers can’t quickly scale production and won’t hold stock without demand certainty. Getting orders and forecasts earlier—farmers and vets committing—would help.\nCat McLaughlin: VMD’s five-year vaccine strategy is welcome. Practical issues matter too: 50–100-dose pack sizes are impractical for smallholders and raise waste concerns.\nCharlie Dewhirst: Was vaccine resale a real problem?\nDr Rees: Anecdotally yes—Facebook Marketplace saw offers—but vaccines need an unbroken cold chain and can lose potency; vets ran broach days to safely share doses at practices.\nAbigail Seager: Manufacturers matched supply to demand. Early this year GB demand seemed insufficient so stocks moved to Europe; once demand rose we brought nearly 5 million doses in rapidly and more are due.\nAbigail Seager: The vaccine products in use had provisional marketing authorisations; that gives safety assurance while manufacturers gather full data, which can limit early detail on some uses.\n\n\n## Lab capacity, APHA transformation and future resilience\n> Bottlenecks came from form-handling, a small specialist APHA verification team and lab submission surges; fixes include digitisation, more lab designation and permanent surge capacity.\n\nChair: To the officials: you all worked hard, but where were the system bottlenecks and how do we fix them before next season?\nProfessor Horton: Pirbright ramped up but faced huge submission volumes. Many sample forms lacked required info, and prioritising pre-movement tests added pressure. We didn’t exceed stated lab PCR capacity but had an early bottleneck.\nRichard Lewis: APHA’s bottleneck was the small Veterinary Exotic Notifiable Diseases Unit that vets to confirm disease and produce certificates. They were swamped checking paper data across multiple systems.\nProfessor Horton: Adding lab partners is possible but requires biosafety licensing, security checks, strict quality assurance and aligned IT systems. Some private labs considered it but found it not financially viable.\nRichard Lewis: We must digitise. APHA will test an online system this November to remove paper forms and automate checks. Our Bridge to Better programme aims to create permanent outbreak capability rather than relying on unsustainable surge.\nJosh Newbury: Could bluetongue ever be eradicated here or will it be recurring?\nProfessor Horton: In theory vaccines can control it; Europe shows comprehensive vaccination reduces impact. Practically, eradication is hard for a vector-borne disease, so preparing for recurring risk is sensible.\nRichard Lewis: The priority is surge capacity and speed: digital reporting, a permanent outbreak team and stronger lab networks. Those investments are disease-agnostic and improve resilience to future threats.\nHelena Dollimore: What about companion-animal emergency cover and practice consolidation—does the Veterinary Surgeons Act reform need to define reasonable travel distances?\nDr Rees: Fixed mileage rules would be hard to apply across remote and urban areas. Reform that regulates businesses and allows justification of cover patterns would be more practical; we can follow up with written detail.\nJosh Newbury: On concurrent threats—could multiple outbreaks overwhelm you?\nRichard Lewis: We already handle multiple threats; Bridge to Better is explicitly designed to cope with several simultaneous incidents and to move beyond short-term surge.\nProfessor Horton: Climate change increases vector-borne risks by speeding up virus replication in insects and expanding insect ranges. We must sustain science capacity and international surveillance to spot threats early.\nChair: Thank you all. Your evidence highlights practical fixes—forecasted vaccine demand, digitised reporting, more lab partners, and a permanent scalable APHA response—plus better trusted communications to raise early vaccine uptake.\n",
